---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SiteNav from '../components/SiteNav.astro';
import SiteFooter from '../components/SiteFooter.astro';
import ContactPanel from '../components/ContactPanel.astro';
import Button from '../components/Button.astro';
import GetInTouch from '../components/GetInTouch.astro';
import { imageAttrs, type SanityImage } from '../sanity/image';
import { COURSE_COMMUNICATION_PAGE_QUERY } from '../sanity/queries';
import { contentParams } from '../sanity/content';
import { PAGE_DEFAULTS, type ImageDefault } from '../sanity/page-defaults';

// The page's headings and photos, from the Communication package page
// document (or the page's defaults); the paragraphs, the details and the
// related products stay here
const page = await Astro.locals.sanity.fetch(COURSE_COMMUNICATION_PAGE_QUERY, contentParams);
const D = PAGE_DEFAULTS.courseCommunicationPage;
// An <img>'s src, size and alt: the photo set in the Studio, or the page's own file
const img = (image: SanityImage | null | undefined, fallback: ImageDefault, width: number) =>
  image?.asset ? { ...imageAttrs(image, width), alt: image.alt ?? fallback.alt } : { src: fallback.path, width: fallback.width, height: fallback.height, alt: fallback.alt };
---
<BaseLayout title="Architecture communication package — Tomrow Studios" namespace="course-communication" pageName="Communication package" pageDoc="courseCommunicationPage">
<SiteNav />


<!-- Header -->
<header class="page-header">
  <img class="page-header-img" data-page-field="header.image" data-page-width="1200" {...img(page?.header?.image, D.header.image, 1200)}>
  <div class="page-header-body">
    <div class="page-header-main">
      <h1 data-page-field="header.heading">{page?.header?.heading ?? D.header.heading}</h1>
      <p class="lead" data-page-field="header.lead">{page?.header?.lead ?? D.header.lead}</p>
      <div class="tags">
        <span class="tag">Templates</span>
        <span class="tag">Digital download</span>
        <span class="tag">Lifetime access</span>
      </div>
    </div>
    <dl class="panel-meta">
      <div><dt>Author</dt><dd>Thomas Rowntree</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Format</dt><dd>PDF and editable files</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Includes</dt><dd>Templates and frameworks</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Buy</dt><dd><a href="#contact" data-panel-open="contact" data-barba-prevent="self" data-scramble-hover="link">Get the package</a></dd></div>
    </dl>
  </div>
</header>

<!-- Overview -->
<section class="section section-alt">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="rich-split">
      <h3 data-page-field="overview.heading">{page?.overview?.heading ?? D.overview.heading}</h3>
      <div class="rich-text">
        <p>Architects are trained to draw and schooled in how buildings go together. Almost nobody is taught to write about what they have made. The result is a profession fluent in one language and hesitant in another, producing project descriptions that read like planning statements and competition texts that give a jury nothing to hold on to.</p>
        <p>This package closes that gap with working documents rather than advice. It gives you the structures behind a project description, a competition narrative, a practice profile and a client-facing summary, each stripped back to the decisions that actually matter and each ready to be filled in with your own work.</p>
        <p>The frameworks are deliberately plain. They ask what the project is for, who it is being explained to, and what a reader needs to understand first. Answer those honestly and the writing follows. Skip them and no amount of adjectives will save the paragraph.</p>
        <p>Everything is editable and yours to keep. Use the templates as they are for a deadline this week, or take them apart and build a house style on top of them.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <img data-click-zoom class="panel-wide-img" data-page-field="overview.image" data-page-width="1200" {...img(page?.overview?.image, D.overview.image, 1200)} loading="lazy" decoding="async">
  </div>
</section>

<!-- What is included -->
<section class="section section-alt">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="rich-split">
      <h3 data-page-field="details.heading">{page?.details?.heading ?? D.details.heading}</h3>
      <div class="rich-text">
        <p>The package is organised around the four documents a practice writes most often. Each comes with a completed worked example, a blank template, and a short set of notes explaining why the structure is shaped the way it is.</p>
        <p>Project descriptions are the workhorse. They appear on your site, in awards submissions, and in every enquiry a prospective client sends. The template separates the brief, the constraint, the move and the outcome, so a reader gets the idea before they get the detail.</p>
        <p>Competition narratives are a different problem. A jury reads dozens in an afternoon and remembers the ones with a single legible argument. The framework here makes you name that argument on the first line and defend it for the rest of the page.</p>

        <ul class="module-list">
          <li><span class="module-name">One</span><span>Project descriptions</span></li>
          <li><span class="module-name">Two</span><span>Competition narratives</span></li>
          <li><span class="module-name">Three</span><span>Practice profiles</span></li>
          <li><span class="module-name">Four</span><span>Client summaries</span></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
    <img data-click-zoom class="panel-wide-img" data-page-field="details.image" data-page-width="1200" {...img(page?.details?.image, D.details.image, 1200)} loading="lazy" decoding="async">
  </div>
</section>

<!-- Related courses — the other three, on the Shop's own card -->
<section class="section section-alt">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="related-courses">
      <div class="related-courses__head">
        <p class="tagline">Also in the shop</p>
        <h2>Related</h2>
        <p>Three more ways to sharpen how the work is drawn, written and shown.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="product-grid">
        <article class="product-card">
          <img class="product-img" src="/assets/photos/thumbnails/thumb-08.jpg" width="1200" height="675" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Architectural presentation masterclass">
          <div class="product-body">
            <div class="product-info">
              <span class="tag tag-dark">Course</span>
              <span class="product-meta">Self-paced</span>
            </div>
            <h5>Architectural presentation masterclass</h5>
            <p>A direct method for producing drawings and presentations that communicate clearly</p>
            <Button href="/product" label="View Course" />
          </div>
        </article>
        <article class="product-card">
          <img class="product-img" src="/assets/photos/lifestyle/lifestyle-10.jpg" width="1200" height="1500" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Architectural Influence">
          <div class="product-body">
            <div class="product-info">
              <span class="tag tag-dark">Book</span>
              <span class="product-meta">Hardcover</span>
            </div>
            <h5>Architectural Influence</h5>
            <p>A precedent analysis of the 21 creators shaping how architecture is seen, shared and understood online</p>
            <Button href="/book" label="Buy the Book" />
          </div>
        </article>
        <article class="product-card">
          <img class="product-img" src="/assets/photos/products/sketchbook.jpg" width="1600" height="1600" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="An Architect's Digital Sketchbook">
          <div class="product-body">
            <div class="product-info">
              <span class="tag tag-dark">Guide</span>
              <span class="product-meta">Free PDF</span>
            </div>
            <h5>An Architect's Digital Sketchbook</h5>
            <p>A step-by-step guide to sketching in Morpholio Trace, with layering and photomontage for portfolio-ready output</p>
            <Button href="/course-sketchbook" label="View Guide" />
          </div>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
<GetInTouch />


<SiteFooter />

<ContactPanel />
</BaseLayout>
